{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-43037","model_call":13,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"solved\" \"russian gas\" europe)"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"solved\" \"russian gas\" europe)"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_1750908065#0_3083862229","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q232","rank":1,"snippet":"<cover>Russian gas giant Gazprom was privatized for $250 million. Three years later, Gazprom's market valuation was $40 billion. Based on its reserves, if it were valued as a company would be in the United States, where property rights are more secure, it would be worth between $300 billion and $900 billion. Oil, mining, electricity and other companies were privatized at prices sometimes less than a twentieth of their subsequent market value. Thus were created the handful of Russian oligarchs who came to dominate the national economy, even as the nation sunk into an economic decline of epic proportions. Rising oil prices have helped the national economy recover since the late 1990s, but the country remains marked by a spectacular concentration of wealth. The country now has 17 billionaires. Writes Paul Klebnikov of Forbes magazine, \"Considering the modest size of the Russian economy these days, Russia may well have the highest billionaire-to-GDP ratio in the world.\" The likely runner up in the billionaire-to-GDP contest is Mexico, which claims 11 spots on Forbes' list of billionaires. As in Russia, the Mexican billionaire class saw its fortunes rise thanks to a series of large-scale privatizations that took </cover>"},"time":46},"model_calls":13,"document_judgments":5,"relevance_3":0,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
